[0.000, "o", ";\u0007), and \u001b[1mMaterial UI\u001b[m\r\n  (\u001b[34;4m\u001b]8;id=jke287bdebe74c3953;file:///Users/brett/Demos/flutter-parts-experiment/parts_tracker_comprehension/lib/ui/workbench_screen.dart\u0007workbench_screen.dart\u001b[m\u001b]8;;\u0007)—all of which map cleanly onto React concepts you already know (components/hooks, immutable\r\n  state & reducers/Zustand/Redux, TypeScript interfaces, and Testing Library).\u001b[K\r\n\u001b[K\n  We'll set up the interactive course inside \u001b[94m./tutorial/\u001b[m so the root project stays untouched. Before I draft\r\n  \u001b[94mtutorial/MISSION.md\u001b[m and the first lesson, let's clarify your goal one step at a time:\u001b[K\r\n\u001b[K\n  \u001b[1mWhat does success look like for you at the end of this course?\u001b[m For example:\r\n\u001b[K\n  1. \u001b[1mRebuild & master this app from the ground up\u001b[m — progressively reconstructing the Parts Tracker in \u001b[94m.\u001b[m\r\n  \u001b[94m/tutorial/apps/\u001b[m (from Dart data models and Riverpod state up to the full workbench UI and widget tests) so you can\r\n  build new Flutter apps with the same stack.\u001b[K\r\n  2. \u001b[1mRead, modify, and e"]
